This is **Partial Prefetching** in Next.js 16. With it on, `<Link>` prefetches only the route's **App Shell** (static + cached content); everything else streams in from behind `<Suspense>`. So put the title in the shell, and the rest inside `<Suspense>`.

**1. Enable the flag** (`next.config.ts`):
```ts
const nextConfig = {
  cacheComponents: true,        // required
  partialPrefetching: true,
}
```

**2. Project page** — title from `params` (URL data) inside `<Suspense>`, but if the title is in your data layer, cache the title fetch:
```tsx
async function getProjectMeta(id: string) {
  'use cache'
  return db.projects.meta(id) // { title, ... }
}

export default async function Page({ params }: PageProps<'/projects/[id]'>) {
  const { id } = await params
  const meta = await getProjectMeta(id) // cached → ships in the App Shell
  return (
    <>
      <h1>{meta.title}</h1>        {/* instant */}
      <Suspense fallback={<BodySkeleton />}>
        <ProjectBody id={id} />   {/* streams in on click */}
      </Suspense>
    </>
  )
}
```
The title is cached and lives in the shared App Shell; the body isn't, so it streams in after navigation.

**3. Link from the dashboard** — keep the default `prefetch`:
```tsx
<Link href={`/projects/${id}`}>{/* default = App Shell only */}</Link>
```

**Incremental option**: instead of the global flag, set `export const prefetch = 'partial'` on the project page itself (see `node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/03-api-reference/03-file-conventions/02-route-segment-config/prefetch.md:38`).

Caveats from the docs: per-link `prefetch={true}` would resolve URL-specific content ahead of the click — only use it on this `<Link>` if you actually want that.

